Class (Blueprint)

A class is a blueprint or model used to define:

properties (data)
methods (functions)

👉 It does not contain real data, it just defines how objects should be.

Example:

class User {
    public $name;

    public function sayHello() {
        echo "Hello " . $this->name;
    }
}
🧍 Object (Instance)

An object is an instance of a class.

👉 It is created using the new keyword and lives in memory (RAM).
👉 It contains real data and can use the class methods.

Example:

$user1 = new User();
$user1->name = "Fatima";
$user1->sayHello();
🗄️ PDO (PHP Data Objects)

PHP PDO

PDO is a built-in PHP class used to interact with databases.

👉 It allows you to:

connect to a database
execute queries (SELECT, INSERT, etc.)
secure your code (prepared statements)

Example:

$pdo = new PDO("mysql:host=localhost;dbname=test", "root", "");

👉 Here:

PDO = class
$pdo = object (instance)



📘 PDO Error Handling & setAttribute – Simple Summary
🔹 Default behavior
By default, PDO does not throw errors on query failures.
Instead, it returns false silently.
This means:
$stmt = $pdo->query("BAD QUERY");
if ($stmt === false) {
    echo "Something went wrong";
}

You must manually check each query for errors.

🔹 setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION)
This changes PDO’s error behavior.
Instead of returning false and being silent:
PDO throws an exception when an error occurs.
Example:
$pdo->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);
🔹 Using try / catch
Wrap your database operations in a try block:
try {
    $pdo->query("BAD QUERY");
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    echo "Error: " . $e->getMessage();
}
✅ If an error occurs:
Program does not crash
Execution goes to catch
You can display or log a custom error message
🔹 Why use ERRMODE_EXCEPTION?
Mode	Behavior
PDO::ERRMODE_SILENT	Returns false (silent)
PDO::ERRMODE_WARNING	Gives PHP warning
PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION	Throws exception (best for control)
Using ERRMODE_EXCEPTION is the recommended way.
It lets you handle errors gracefully instead of letting the program crash.
🔹 Summary in one line

setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION) ensures PDO throws exceptions on errors, 
allowing you to catch, control,and display errors safely, instead of silently returning false.